      <article class="full-article">
        <p class="story-kicker">BUSINESS</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Tech CEO Apologizes for Calling Everything &#x27;AI&#x27; When It&#x27;s Just Math</h1>
        <p class="article-dek">Statement promises future products will be &#x27;at least partially real.</p>
        <p class="article-meta">By Marcus Tweed · Wednesday, August 19, 2026 · SAN FRANCISCO · 3 min read</p>
        
<div class="article-share" aria-label="Share this story">
  <span class="article-share-label">Share</span>
  <a href="mailto:?subject=Tech%20CEO%20Apologizes%20for%20Calling%20Everything%20%27AI%27%20When%20It%27s%20Just%20Math&amp;body=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/tech-ceo-apologizes-for-calling-everything-ai-when-its-just-math/">Email</a>
  <a href="https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/tech-ceo-apologizes-for-calling-everything-ai-when-its-just-math/&amp;text=Tech%20CEO%20Apologizes%20for%20Calling%20Everything%20%27AI%27%20When%20It%27s%20Just%20Math" rel="noopener">Post</a>
  <a href="../../reprints.html">Reprints</a>
</div>
        
        <figure class="article-hero article-hero-fit-cover">
          <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40?auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;w=800&amp;h=500&amp;q=80" alt="" />
        </figure>
        <div class="ad-slot ad-slot-in-content" data-ad-slot="inContent"></div>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>SAN FRANCISCO - NeuraStack CEO Gil Marr issued a public apology Thursday for describing every company feature as artificial intelligence, including a sorting algorithm he admitted was &quot;mostly if statements wearing a turtleneck.&quot; The mea culpa followed investor pressure and a viral thread documenting NeuraStack&#x27;s &quot;neural laundry mode,&quot; which simply runs hot water twice.</p>
<p>&quot;We confused branding with cognition,&quot; Marr said during the press conference. &quot;Our AI toothbrush tracks brushing patterns. That is a timer. We should not have given it a personality.&quot; The audience murmured in understanding, some even chuckling at the absurdity of the claim.</p>
<p>The apology came as NeuraStack faced scrutiny from both investors and tech critics alike. One investor, Sarah Chen, said, &quot;We invested in NeuraStack for its cutting-edge AI solutions, not for your &#x27;AI toothbrush&#x27; that&#x27;s just a fancy timer.&quot; The company’s stock dipped 2%, but Marr quickly rebounded by saying the apology itself was &quot;AI-assisted,&quot; which he clarified meant using Grammarly to polish his statement.</p>
<p>Engineers present at the press conference received applause for their candidness. Lead developer Renee Kwan demonstrated the actual model powering customer support: a decision tree and one intern named Kyle. The audience laughed, but Marr took it in stride, saying, &quot;We&#x27;re working on integrating more AI into our operations.&quot;</p>
<p>Marr pledged to add transparency labels ranking products from True Model to Vibes Only. Early adopters of NeuraStack’s AI Pet Rock received refunds and a sincere note. &quot;We’re sorry for the confusion,&quot; read the note. &quot;Your pet rock is not actually an AI, but we hope you enjoyed it anyway.&quot;</p>
<p>Industry analysts expected limited behavior change sector-wide. Dr. Amir Qasim said, &quot;Apologies are the new roadmap.&quot; He added, &quot;Wait six weeks for the next announcement. By then, everyone will have forgotten about this one and moved on to the next big thing.&quot;</p>
<p>The company’s PR team scrambled to manage the fallout. They released a statement emphasizing that NeuraStack was committed to genuine AI innovation. However, the damage had already been done. A viral tweet from tech blogger Alex Chen read: &quot;NeuraStack&#x27;s &#x27;AI&#x27; laundry mode is just a fancy way of saying it uses hot water twice.&quot;</p>
<p>Kyle, the intern behind the decision tree, declined comment but updated his LinkedIn profile to Architect of Honesty. The change was met with mixed reactions - some praised him for his integrity, while others wondered if he would be next in line for an apology.</p>
<p>Marr concluded the press conference by saying, &quot;We’re going to need a bigger boat,&quot; as the room erupted into laughter. He then clarified that it was a reference to the famous scene from The Titanic, adding, &quot;But this time, we won’t sink.&quot;</p>
<p>As Marr walked off stage, he received a round of applause for his ability to turn an apology into a PR opportunity. The audience left with mixed feelings - some were still chuckling at the absurdity of it all, while others wondered if NeuraStack would ever regain its reputation as a serious player in the AI industry.</p>
<p>In the end, NeuraStack’s stock recovered slightly when Marr said, &quot;Our apology was AI-assisted,&quot; which he clarified meant using Grammarly. The company promised to be more transparent and genuine going forward, but many remained skeptical about their ability to maintain such honesty in the future.</p>
          
        </div>
        
